derivations.claim:
Put $(C_1,\ldots,C_5)=(B,A,O,A,B)$, $(c_1,\ldots,c_5)=(1/2,1/2,1,1/2,1/2)$, and $\Psi(u,z)=(P_{c_1u}^{C_1}\cdots P_{c_5u}^{C_5}\phi)(z)$. The subflow integral identity $P_s^C\psi-\psi=\int_0^sP_v^C C\psi\,dv$ follows from the explicit A/B maps and Itô's formula for O. It and the weighted derivative bounds in step 1.1 justify differentiating this five-factor product three times, with right derivatives at zero. Explicitly, its third derivative is the finite ordered sum. [F1, F2, F3, given, step 1.1, algebra]
$$\partial_u^3\Psi(u)=\sum_{
u_1+\cdots+
u_5=3} rac{3!\prod_{i=1}^5c_i^{
u_i}}{\prod_{i=1}^5
u_i!} igl(P_{c_1u}^{C_1}C_1^{
u_1}\cdots P_{c_5u}^{C_5}C_5^{
u_5}\phi igr).$$
Every summand has total generator length three, so step 1.1 bounds it by $CW_{p+3}(z)$; no term with more than three generators is formed. The first derivative at zero is $L\phi$. Grouping the second derivative at zero gives the pure squares $A^2+B^2+O^2$ and each ordered mixed pair $AB,BA,AO,OA,BO,OB$ once, hence $\partial_u^2\Psi(0)=L^2\phi$. Taylor's formula with its third-derivative integral remainder now gives
$$K_h\phi=\phi+hL\phi+	frac12h^2L^2\phi+	frac12\int_0^h(h-u)^2\partial_u^3\Psi(u)\,du,$$
whose remainder is bounded by $Ch^3W_{p+3}(z)$. This is a second-order Taylor expansion of the product itself, rather than multiplication of five truncated polynomials with higher differential cross-products.
